{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"0b512d91-a889-4f41-b80a-5ca51cca0514","step_index":14,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"call_mcp_tool","duration_seconds":0.596861,"tool_info":{"name":"call_mcp_tool","parameters":{"Arguments":{"slug":"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-"},"ServerName":"worklore_worklore","ToolName":"get_story"},"output":"{\n  \"slug\": \"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-\",\n  \"title\": \"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\",\n  \"capability\": {\n    \"tier\": \"T0\",\n    \"summary\": \"T0 · inert — touches nothing\",\n    \"tier_label\": \"Inert — no scanned capability strings; instructional text only\",\n    \"sha256\": \"ee118776c3379edb003712d00d2538c6059004c318b5c5686eac4b7e6bdc8e7c\",\n    \"findings\": [],\n    \"note\": \"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"\n  },\n  \"markdown\": \"---\\ntitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\ndate: 2026-04-15\\ntags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\\ntype: success\\nstatus: n/a\\nreproducible: true\\n---\\n\\n# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\n\\nI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\\nIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\\ninstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\\nauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\\nand none of them are documented in one place.\\n\\n**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\\nno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\\nathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\\nDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\\non the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\\n\\\"reconnect\\\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\\n\\n**2. `\\\"Duplicate scope\\\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\\narea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\\nother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\\nmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\\n\\n**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\\nonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\\n`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\\nsaves you the \\\"who am I\\\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\\npath anyway.\\n\\n**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\\nendpoint as GET: a plain `\u003ca href=\\\"/api/intervals/auth/init\\\"\u003e` link that\\nwould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\\na full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\\nkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\\ninit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\\nthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\\n\\nWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\\neither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\\nthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\\n15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\\naccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\\ndiffers from the one already stored.\\n\\n## Reproduce this\\n\\nPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\\nin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\\nIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\\n[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\\n(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\\nNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\\n\\nYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\\nthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\\nsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\\n\\nSteps:\\n\\n1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\\n   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...\u0026redirect_uri=...\u0026scope=...\u0026state=...`\\n   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\\n   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\\n   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\\n2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\\n   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\\n3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \\\"intervals_oauth\\\", exp:\\n   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\\n   it belongs to.\\n4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\\n   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\\n   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\\n   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\\n   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\\n5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e` against\\n   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\\n   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\\n\\nVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\\ndata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\\n401, and your app must degrade to \\\"reconnect\\\" instead of crashing. Finally,\\nrequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\\nmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\\n\",\n  \"after_you_apply_this\": \"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"\n}"}}}
